#!spectest
# mode: run
# adjudicator: production-v1
# since: spec-v0.1
# features: [move, callable]
# doc: |
#   RV-F #15 (single-owner Callable, REJECT direction — the struct-ctor position).
#   A bare `Callable` identifier passed to a struct constructor field init is an
#   implicit copy of a single-owner value — E_MoveWithoutOperator (write `!f` /
#   `f.clone()`). This pins the ctor arm of production's single-owner-init
#   carve-out (a param is NOT exempt at ctor sites, unlike a bare bind). The
#   callable field is only constructed, never called (`.cb()`-style field call is
#   outside ggdef's phase-0 subset — the reject fires at the ctor before any call).
#
#   adjudicator: production-v1 — ELABORATE-stage reject (GGDEF-SKIP on the ggdef
#   run-surface lane; C/LLVM affirm error[E_MoveWithoutOperator] at build).
#
#   KNOWN SELF-HOST GAP (filed, TODO.md): the self-host does not treat `Callable`
#   as single-owner, so it ACCEPTS this ctor init — the self-host lane MISMATCHes.
#   The expected verdict is what the language SHOULD do (reject); the fixture
#   EXPOSES the gap.
# expect:
#   exit: 1
#   stdout: ""
#   reject: E_MoveWithoutOperator
#!end

struct Holder:
    Callable[int(int)] fn
    int tag

void main():
    Callable[int(int)] f = (n): n * 2
    Holder h = Holder(f, 7)
    print(h.tag)
